# Emby SSO licence service - example configuration
#
#   cp service/.env.example service/.env
#   chmod 600 service/.env
#   # fill in the blanks, then:
#   docker compose up -d --build licence
#
# The values below are the ones a real first run on a real host was verified
# with, except the secrets, which are blank. See docs/first-run.md.
#
# NEVER COMMIT THE .env YOU MAKE FROM THIS. It is gitignored; keep it that way.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Required
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# SELF-SERVICE SIGNING. Set this and the service signs licences itself the
# moment a customer activates - one press of Activate and they have a licence.
#
# What it costs, precisely: the private key is loaded by the process that
# answers the internet. It mints a valid licence for ANY Emby server, forever,
# and there is no revocation, so a break-in here takes the whole scheme with it
# and does so silently.
#
# Leave it UNSET and the service cannot sign at all. Activations queue, and you
# sign them at /admin/signing with `licencetool sign` on a machine that answers
# no requests. Safer. Not instant. Both are supported; this is the trade.
#
# The file must be chmod 600 and owned by uid 5678, or the service will not
# start.
LICENCE_SIGNING_KEY_PATH=/run/secrets/licence-signing-key.private.json

# The PUBLIC half of the RELEASE key - the one that signs plugin updates. NOT
# the licence key, and that separation is the point: the licence key is on this
# host, so if it could also sign releases, a break-in here would mean pushing a
# backdoored DLL to every customer's Emby server. The release key lives on your
# own machine and never comes here.
#
# Only used to check a manifest before publishing it on /admin/release, so a
# manifest signed with the wrong key is caught on your screen rather than by
# every customer. Not a secret. Unset means the Release page cannot accept
# anything; everything else works.
LICENCE_RELEASE_PUBLIC_KEYS=

# The PUBLIC key or keys the plugin build trusts: one JWK, or a JSON array of
# them. NOT a secret - it is the same value that is compiled into
# src/Emby.Sso/Protocol/LicencePublicKey.cs, and `licencetool keygen` printed
# it. It is what lets this service check a signed licence before storing it, so
# a wrong key or a wrong file is caught on your screen rather than on somebody
# else's server. The service refuses to start if it carries private material.
LICENCE_PUBLIC_KEYS={"kty":"RSA","n":"REPLACE-WITH-THE-VALUE-IN-LicencePublicKey.cs","e":"AQAB"}

# Inside the container, on the mounted volume. Holds the SQLite store, the
# ledger of who bought what, and the outbox. BACK IT UP.
LICENCE_DATA_DIR=/data

# The address the PLUGIN has compiled in. If these disagree, buy links point
# nowhere and activation fails. Note the spelling: license, not licence.
# Also the address plugin updates are served from (this plus
# /v1/release/download), and therefore what a release manifest is signed for.
LICENCE_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://license.koper.cloud

# sandbox until a real purchase has been tested end to end.
PAYPAL_ENV=sandbox

# From the PayPal developer dashboard, on the webhook you created. PayPal signs
# it into every message, so a wrong value fails every webhook CLOSED - which is
# the safe direction, and also means "nothing happens" is the symptom.
PAYPAL_WEBHOOK_ID=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Required to actually sell. Without them the service still runs and codes can
# still be issued by hand; /buy just says "not set up to take payments yet".
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

PAYPAL_CLIENT_ID=
PAYPAL_CLIENT_SECRET=

PAYPAL_CURRENCY=EUR
PAYPAL_PRICE=19.00

# The floor a captured payment must clear. Defaults to PAYPAL_PRICE, so set it
# only to allow something lower deliberately. It exists because without a floor
# a captured payment of any size - a penny - would buy a full licence.
# PAYPAL_MINIMUM_AMOUNT=19.00

# PAYPAL_PRODUCT_NAME=Emby SSO plugin licence
# PAYPAL_RETURN_URL=       # defaults to LICENCE_PUBLIC_BASE_URL/buy/complete
# PAYPAL_CANCEL_URL=       # defaults to LICENCE_PUBLIC_BASE_URL/buy/cancelled

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Licence shape (defaults shown)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# How many DIFFERENT Emby servers one code may be activated onto. Re-activating
# onto a server it already holds is free and does not count.
# LICENCE_ACTIVATIONS_ALLOWED=3

# How long an issued licence lasts. There is NO REVOCATION - the plugin checks
# offline and never calls home - so this is the only control you have over a
# licence once it exists. Choose deliberately.
# LICENCE_DAYS=365

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rate limiting on /v1/activate (defaults shown)
#
# Redemption codes are bearer secrets guessed over the internet and this
# endpoint cannot be authenticated, so this is the brake. A real user activates
# once; these numbers only bite a script.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# LICENCE_RATE_PER_CLIENT_PER_MINUTE=10
# LICENCE_RATE_PER_CLIENT_BURST=5
# LICENCE_RATE_GLOBAL_PER_MINUTE=300
# LICENCE_RATE_MAX_TRACKED_CLIENTS=20000

# How many reverse proxies sit in front of this service. 0 means the socket's
# peer address IS the client. Set it WRONG-HIGH and a caller can spoof their own
# address through X-Forwarded-For and walk around the rate limit above.
# LICENCE_TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The admin page at /admin. ALL OPTIONAL - and there is NO PAGE AT ALL unless
# ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH (or ADMIN_PASSWORD) is set. Not a login form, not a 401:
# the routes are never mapped and /admin answers 404 like any other path.
#
# READ service/README.md, "The admin page", BEFORE TURNING THIS ON. It is a
# door on the public internet in front of the box holding your signing key.
# Whoever gets through it can issue a licence for any server, forever, and
# nothing can recall one. The password is the entire barrier.
#
# Make the value with:  licence hash-password   (it reads stdin, so the
# password does not reach your shell history or the process list)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH=pbkdf2-sha256.210000.<salt>.<hash>

# The plaintext alternative. Second best: anything that can read this
# container's environment gets the credential itself rather than a verifier.
# Refused if it is under 16 characters or obvious.
# ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# ADMIN_SESSION_IDLE_MINUTES=30       # signed out after this long doing nothing
# ADMIN_SESSION_ABSOLUTE_MINUTES=480  # signed out after this long regardless

# A wrong password buys a wait that DOUBLES from the first number up to the
# second. There is deliberately no lockout: you are the only person who could
# lift one, so an attacker who could trigger it could lock you out of your own
# service for free.
# ADMIN_LOGIN_DELAY_SECONDS=2
# ADMIN_LOGIN_MAX_DELAY_SECONDS=60

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Emailing the code. ALL OPTIONAL - off unless SMTP_HOST is set.
#
# Off is a working configuration: paid codes land in /data/codes-outbox.jsonl
# and you send them by hand. A failed send NEVER fails the webhook.
# See docs/email-delivery-checklist.md before trusting a real send.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# SMTP_HOST=smtp.example.com
# SMTP_FROM_ADDRESS=licences@example.com
# SMTP_USERNAME=licences@example.com
# SMTP_PASSWORD=

# One of exactly three words:
#   tls        implicit TLS, encrypted from the first byte, usually port 465
#   starttls   plain then MUST upgrade, fails rather than continuing in the
#              clear, usually 587. THE DEFAULT.
#   none       no encryption. Only for a relay on this host. Setting a username
#              with this refuses to start, because that puts the relay password
#              on the wire.
# SMTP_SECURITY=starttls
# SMTP_PORT=587            # defaults to 465/587/25 to match SMTP_SECURITY

# SMTP_FROM_NAME=Emby SSO licences
# SMTP_REPLY_TO=help@example.com
# SMTP_SUBJECT=Your Emby SSO plugin licence code
# SMTP_SUPPORT_CONTACT=help@example.com
# SMTP_TEMPLATE_PATH=/run/config/licence-email.txt   # must contain {code}
# SMTP_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=30
# SMTP_MAX_ATTEMPTS=4      # then the outbox is the fallback
# SMTP_RETRY_SECONDS=30    # first backoff, quadrupled each attempt

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Encrypted backups - optional, and the thing to turn on first
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# With this set, /admin/backup hands you an encrypted copy of everything on the
# volume that cannot be rebuilt: who bought what, which servers are activated,
# every licence signed so far, the outbox and the audit trail. Nothing anywhere
# else can reconstruct it - not PayPal, not the plugin, not the signing machine.
#
# There is no unencrypted option. That store holds redemption codes and live
# licences, and the point of a backup is to put it somewhere less careful than
# this box. At least 16 characters; the service refuses to start under that.
#
#   openssl rand -base64 32
#
# KEEP IT SOMEWHERE OTHER THAN BESIDE THE BACKUPS, and do not change it
# casually: reading a backup back needs the passphrase that was in force when it
# was TAKEN, so a change strands every backup made before it.
LICENCE_BACKUP_PASSPHRASE=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# In front of the admin password - optional, both off by default
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# /admin is on the public internet and the password is otherwise the only
# barrier to your customer list. It is a good password, hashed with 210,000
# rounds of PBKDF2 and throttled on failure - and it is one factor, held by one
# person, on machines that also read email.
#
# Both of these fail CLOSED when set, and a request that fails either gets a
# 404: the page does not exist rather than refusing, so somebody scanning for
# admin panels learns nothing, including that there is one here.
#
# A comma-separated list of networks the admin page may be reached from. A bare
# address means that host. THIS DEPENDS ON LICENCE_TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS being
# right - behind a proxy with it at 0, every request looks like it came from the
# proxy and either everyone or nobody is allowed. Set the hop count first and
# check the log before relying on this.
# ADMIN_ALLOWED_CIDRS=203.0.113.4/32, 10.0.0.0/8

# A header your own proxy adds, checked in constant time and BEFORE the password
# - so a caller who cannot produce it never reaches a PBKDF2 verification. This
# is where a Cloudflare Access assertion, an oauth2-proxy header, a client
# certificate your proxy verified, or simply a long shared secret plugs in.
#
# YOUR PROXY MUST STRIP THIS HEADER FROM INCOMING REQUESTS. A header a client can
# set is not a check, and nothing in this service can enforce that for you.
# ADMIN_REQUIRED_HEADER=X-From-The-Proxy
# ADMIN_REQUIRED_HEADER_VALUE=
